A60 — a pointer that reports positions moves the cursor to them

The host sends input through a virtual pointer (I1). That protocol has two
motion requests: a step (motion) and a position (motion_absolute). Only the step
was heard, so the host turned every touch position into a step from the cursor
position it kept for itself. gamescope keeps the cursor inside the focused
window's surface; a step that ran past an edge was cut short here and not there,
and from then on every touch landed that far off. The same happened whenever the
cursor moved without the host: a window that takes the focus, a game that warps
the pointer, a window smaller than the nested display.

The position is now heard too. It is a place on the output, 0..1 on each axis,
and it reaches the focused window as a touch does: through the window's offset
and scale on the output, then clamped to the surface. There is no second cursor
to disagree with.

The games that read steps still get them. The step sent with a position is the
one from the cursor to it, and a confinement bounds it as it bounds a mouse. A
locked pointer does not move, so there the step is the one from the last
position: a drag turns the view as a mouse drag does.
